{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BoygPrH2kPRSaZMVuaXDfS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have found where the Merkle tree is built! The key function is `MerkleTreeGpu::<F, MH::Digest>::new_with_hash::<MH>` which is defined in `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs` (which we saw earlier). \n\nBased on my thorough investigation, here's what I found:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've analyzed the GPU performance optimization for the stark-backend project. Here are my key findings:\n\n### Kernel Performance Data\nFrom the nsight profiling logs, the Merkle tree construction involves three main kernels:\n\n1. **`calculate_zero_hash` kernel** — 584 instances\n   - **Location**: Unknown (not found in visible codebase)\n   - **Performance**: 3.7% total time = ~109ms, 182μs per call on average (182,305ns median)\n   - **Characteristics**: Called 584 times with remarkably consistent timing (182-198μs)\n\n2. **`merkle_tree_compress` kernel** — 64 instances  \n   - **Location**: Unknown (not found in visible codebase)\n   - **Performance**: 2.6% total time = ~74.9ms, ~1.17ms per call on average\n   - **Characteristics**: Similar consistent timing pattern (3,920-4,197ns minimum/maximum range)\n\n3. **`merkle_tree_init` kernel** — 2 instances\n   - **Location**: Unknown (not found in visible codebase)\n   - **Performance**: 2.4% total time = ~70.8ms, ~35.4ms per call\n   - **Characteristics**: Very large per-call time, only 2 instances\n\n### Merkle Tree Construction Logic\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs` (lines 81-161)\n\nThe actual visible Merkle tree implementation uses these kernels (called from Rust):\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu` (lines 16-79):\n  - `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` - compresses rows into leaf hashes\n  - `poseidon2_strided_compress_layer_kernel` - compresses digest pairs to b