       z_pow = z_pow.square();\n                }\n            }\n\n            let d_z0_pows2 = z0_pows2.to_device()?;\n            let d_z_pows2 = z_pows2.to_device()?;\n            unsafe {\n                w_moments_accumulate(\n                    &mut w_moments,\n                    &d_z0_pows2,\n                    &d_z_pows2,\n                    gamma,\n                    num_queries as u32,\n                    log_height,\n                )\n                .map_err(|error| WhirProverError::WMomentsAccumulate { error, whir_round })?;\n            }\n        }\n\n        m -= k_whir;\n        log_rs_domain_size -= 1;\n    }\n\n    mem.emit_metrics();\n    Ok(WhirProof {\n        mu_pow_witness,\n        whir_sumcheck_polys,\n        codeword_commits,\n        ood_values,\n        folding_pow_witnesses,\n        query_phase_pow_witnesses,\n        initial_round_opened_rows,\n        initial_round_merkle_proofs,\n        codeword_opened_values,\n        codeword_merkle_proofs,\n        final_poly: final_poly.unwrap(),","numLines":40,"startLine":490,"totalLines":718}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qad5uzGPhTwNE2XvS9ByYP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Line 513: `m -= k_whir;`\nLine 514: `log_rs_domain_size -= 1;`\n\nSo `m` is updated after each WHIR round. With k_whir=4, `m` decreases by 4 each round.\n\nFor the buffer allocation pattern:\n- WHIR round 0:\n  - Inner rounds 0-3: f_height goes from 2^m to 2^(m-3), allocating 2^(m-1) to 2^(m-4) sized buffers\n  - After: m becomes m - 4\n- WHIR round 1:\n  - Inner rounds 0-3: f_height goes from 2^(m-4) to 2^(m-7), allocating 2^(m-5) to 2^(m-8) sized buffers\n  - After: m decreases by 4 again\n- WHIR round 2 (final):\n  - Inner rounds 0-3: f_height ranges from 2^(m-8) to 2^(m-11)\n\nWith m=24 and k_whir=4, I can trace through how the buffer sizes shrink across all three WHIR rounds.\n\nLooking at the memory allocations with _final_poly_len around 12, I get 3 total WHIR rounds. The first round starts with 256 MiB allocations that halve with each inner round down to 32 MiB, then the second round continues from 16 MiB and drops to 8 MiB where it transitions to regular CUDA memory instead of the virtual page manager.\n\nThe remaining rounds all stay under 16 MiB and use standard async allocation. Across the VPMM rounds, I'm doing 10 allocations and 10 frees total, which works out to 62 pages needed across both buffers. In theory that's 24.8ms of overhead from page creation, but in practice the pages get recycled from the pool after the initial rounds, so the first allocation needs 32 new pages but subsequent rounds reuse those freed pages.\n\nThe real cost comes from the mapping and unmapping operations—each allocation and deallocation pair takes roughly 100-200 microseconds, which adds up to about 2ms across all 20 operations. There's also some defragmentation overhead happening in the VPMM layer that I need to account for.\n\nLooking at the actual performance data, the VPMM pool state sensitivity is causing much larger regressions—50 to 200 milliseconds in some cases. The 25ms regression we're seeing in WHIR aligns with when the VPMM page size changes, which suggests that pre-allocating the fold buffers could stabilize the pool state and eliminate that regression. The real win here isn't just avoiding the page creation overhead, but preventing the memory pool from becoming fragmented and destabilized by the allocation churn. If I can keep the pool state consistent across rounds, I could recover 15-25ms at APC 300.\n\nI should check whether APC 100 has a similar WHIR regression that could benefit from the same fix. Now I'll draft the task and implementation plan, and I need to look at the WHIR CUDA kernel to understand what the fold function expects.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1429,"cache_read_input_tokens":186831,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1429},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"719c141a-1a84-4f89-a64a-f54435a4c1b4","uuid":"622279d2-ccb2-4914-b283-a07545cc36d4"}
