{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qad5uzGPhTwNE2XvS9ByYP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Njm4UteY2CC2o16VJMZ8uw","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-15-2230-prealloc-whir-fold-buffers\n\nPre-allocate and reuse WHIR sumcheck fold buffers (`f_coeffs`, `w_moments`) via a pingpong pattern instead of allocating new DeviceBuffers each inner round. Currently, each of the k_whir × num_whir_rounds inner sumcheck rounds allocates two fresh DeviceBuffers (one for folded `f_coeffs`, one for folded `w_moments`) and frees the previous pair. For the first ~5 rounds at APC 300 (buffer sizes 128-16 MiB), these go through VPMM, creating page churn that disturbs GPU memory pool state for subsequent bandwidth-bound phases. The WHIR phase regressed 25ms from baseline (100ms → 125ms) coinciding with VPMM page size changes, suggesting pool state sensitivity. Pre-allocating at maximum size and alternating between two buffer pairs eliminates all per-round VPMM allocations/frees and stabilizes pool state.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1429,"cache_read_input_tokens":186831,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1429},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"719c141a-1a84-4f89-a64a-f54435a4c1b4","uuid":"509d9d3c-3a5e-441c-9825-cc37852e9a82"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Njm4UteY2CC2o16VJMZ8uw","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"719c141a-1a84-4f89-a64a-f54435a4c1b4","uuid":"ce167d53-3ed0-4120-bc1f-4b664665fdc3","timestamp":"2026-04-15T20:10:51.343Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-15-2230-prealloc-whir-fold-buffers\n\nPre-allocate and reuse WHIR sumcheck fold buffers (`f_coeffs`, `w_moments`) via a pingpong pattern instead of allocating new DeviceBuffers each inner round. Currently, each of the k_whir × num_whir_rounds inner sumcheck rounds allocates two fresh DeviceBuffers (one for folded `f_coeffs`, one for folded `w_moments`) and frees the previous pair. For the first ~5 rounds at APC 300 (buffer sizes 128-16 MiB), these go through VPMM, creating page churn that disturbs GPU memory pool state for subsequent bandwidth-bound phases. The WHIR phase regressed 25ms from baseline (100ms → 125ms) coinciding with VPMM page size changes, suggesting pool state sensitivity. Pre-allocating at maximum size and alternating between two buffer pairs eliminates all per-round VPMM allocations/frees and stabilizes pool state.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-15-2100-precompute-mle-fold-layout","+# 2026-04-15-2230-prealloc-whir-fold-buffers"," ","-MLE Rounds is the most anti-scaling STARK component: 166ms at APC 300 vs 118ms at APC 0 (0.71x ratio, worse at APC 300 despite 2.35x fewer cells). The `fold_mle_evals` function calls `fold_pingpong` twice per round (mat_evals + selectors), each rebuilding 4 descriptor arrays via `to_device()` and launching a separate kernel — totaling 112 `to_device()` calls and 28 kernel launches per segment across 14 rounds. Pre-computing all fold descriptors once and bulk-uploading before the MLE loop, plus fusing the two per-round fold calls into a single kernel launch, eliminates 104 CUDA API calls and halves fold launches. Expected savings: 5-15ms at APC 300. The fold descriptor overhead is known to be small (~0.3-0.5μs per CUDA API call from prior measurement), so this is a modest improvement that primarily reduces noise in the MLE overhead budget.","+Pre-allocate and reuse WHIR sumcheck fold buffers (`f_coeffs`, `w_moments`) via a pingpong pattern instead of allocating new DeviceBuffers each inner round. Currently, each of the k_whir × num_whir_rounds inner sumcheck rounds allocates two fresh DeviceBuffers (one for folded `f_coeffs`, one for folded `w_moments`) and frees the previous pair. For the first ~5 rounds at APC 300 (buffer sizes 128-16 MiB), these go through VPMM, creating page churn that disturbs GPU memory pool state for subsequent bandwidth-bound phases. The WHIR phase regressed 25ms from baseline (100ms → 125ms) coinciding with VPMM page size changes, suggesting pool state sensitivity. Pre-allocating at maximum size and alternating between two buffer pairs eliminates all per-round VPMM allocations/frees and stabilizes pool state."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-15-2100-precompute-mle-fold-layout\n\nMLE Rounds is the most anti-scaling STARK component: 166ms at APC 300 vs 118ms at APC 0 (0.71x ratio, worse at APC 300 despite 2.35x fewer cells). The `fold_mle_evals` function calls `fold_pingpong` twice per round (mat_evals + selectors), each rebuilding 4 descriptor arrays via `to_device()` and launching a separate kernel — totaling 112 `to_device()` calls and 28 kernel launches per segment across 14 rounds. Pre-computing all fold descriptors once and bulk-uploading before the MLE loop, plus fusing the two per-round fold calls into a single kernel launch, eliminates 104 CUDA API calls and halves fold launches. Expected savings: 5-15ms at APC 300. The fold descriptor overhead is known to be small (~0.3-0.5μs per CUDA API