tched capacities for the next iteration, so the fold operation could write beyond the allocated bounds of whichever buffer ends up as the destination.\n\nLet me trace through what happens across rounds: both buffers start pre-allocated at 2^24, and as long as they maintain that capacity, the fold can safely write its shrinking output (2^23, then 2^22, etc.) to either one. The key insight is that pre-allocating both buffers at the maximum size upfront prevents any capacity violations, regardless of how the swap shuffles them between rounds.\n\nThe real problem surfaces after the inner loop finishes—w_moments_accumulate needs the right-sized buffer, which the \"allocate on the last round\" approach handles. But I'm realizing the swap pattern itself creates a dependency on whether k_whir is odd or even, so let me refactor this to use explicit ping-pong buffers instead of swapping, which will make the state clearer.\n\nRefactoring with explicit ping-pong buffers... Still writing the folding logic... I'm realizing the borrow checker will fight me on this approach with multiple buffers and runtime state. Let me step back and use a cleaner strategy: pre-allocate one extra buffer pair at maximum capacity before the outer loop, then reuse it for the first k_whir-1 inner rounds by alternating which buffer receives the fold results.\n\nBut this still leaves me with the same problem—after all the rounds, the result buffers have their original capacity but the logical data is smaller. The only real solution is to find a way to resize the logical length of a DeviceBuffer without reallocating, or check if the existing implementation already supports this through the non-owning buffer views that were added earlier. I'm realizing this approach is getting too complex, so let me step back and focus on what's actually achievable here. The cleaner strategy is to pre-allocate one extra buffer pair and reuse it for the first k_whir-1 inner rounds, then allocate fresh buffers only for the final inner round when the sizes need to be exact. This cuts allocations by three-quarters per outer round—across all four WHIR rounds, that's roughly 24 fewer allocations total.\n\nThe beauty of this is it sidesteps the w_moments_accumulate sizing issue entirely since the last round produces correctly-sized output, keeps the implementation straightforward with a simple swap pattern, and plays nicely with Rust's ownership model. Let me refactor the code with this approach.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3658,"cache_read_input_tokens":195810,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3658},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"719c141a-1a84-4f89-a64a-f54435a4c1b4","uuid":"11a5a23f-0de3-4d2e-9324-dd8c216f783f"}
